Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.520
THE APP STORE:
In 2021, the Apple App Store brought in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:10.840
total of $85 billion worth of revenue out of which&nbsp;
Apple’s cut of is about $20 to $25 billion. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:17.800
itself is insane, but if we look at it in terms of&nbsp;
profit, things become even crazier. Historically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:25.280
the App Store has boasted an operating margin of&nbsp;
78% meaning that $17.5 billion of this is pure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:31.120
profit. For perspective, Apple as a whole nets&nbsp;
$114 billion per year meaning that the App Store&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:37.000
alone accounts for over 15% of Apple’s entire&nbsp;
bottom line. Apple currently boasts a valuation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:44.200
of just under $3 trillion with $114 billion worth&nbsp;
of profit. So, with $17.5 billion worth of profit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:50.640
you could argue that the App Store itself is worth&nbsp;
roughly $460 billion. That would make the iOS App&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:55.800
Store the 15th largest company in the world.&nbsp;
Even if you want to be super conservative and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.800 --> 00:01:01.760
cut that valuation in half, we’re still talking&nbsp;
about a market cap of $230 billion which would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:07.240
still make the App Store roughly the same size&nbsp;
as Coca Cola or Toyota. And when you zoom out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:13.040
this is really not all that surprising given that&nbsp;
the App Store was responsible for $1.1 trillion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:19.680
worth of transactions in 2022. For perspective,&nbsp;
even Amazon only boasts half that revenue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:26.280
but forget companies. With $1.1 trillion worth of&nbsp;
essentially GDP value, the App Store would rank as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:33.680
the 17th largest country by GDP. But it wasn’t&nbsp;
an easy journey getting here. In fact, Apple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:38.560
initially didn’t even have plans of launching&nbsp;
such an App store, and Steve Jobs insistence of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:43.440
doing everything the Apple way has caused quite&nbsp;
a bit of trouble for Apple in regards to the App&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:56.298
Store. So here’s the full story of the Apple App&nbsp;
Store and how powerful the App Store really is.

00:01:56.298 --> 00:01:57.680
ACCIDENTIAL INVENTION:
With how successful the app&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:03.480
store has become, you probably think that this was&nbsp;
always part of Apple and more specifically Steve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:09.880
Jobs’ grand vision for the iPhone, but that’s not&nbsp;
actually the case. In fact, the original iPhone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:14.680
didn’t even have an app store, and while Apple&nbsp;
was working on an app store in the background,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:19.560
it wasn’t meant to be something big. It was&nbsp;
just supposed to be a place where users could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:24.720
download auxiliary apps from Apple themselves.&nbsp;
In fact, Apple had no intention of letting 3rd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:29.840
party developers onto the App Store at all. Steve&nbsp;
Jobs figured that developers could just create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:34.480
web apps that could be run on Safari. This would&nbsp;
be easier for developers to adapt their existing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:39.840
platforms and easier for Apple as they wouldn’t&nbsp;
have to police the app store. But developers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:44.840
weren’t exactly a massive fan of this idea. They&nbsp;
would protest on forums and purposefully avoid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:49.600
creating mobile versions of their web apps. But,&nbsp;
what really pushed things over the edge was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:54.960
jailbreaking community. Just a few days after the&nbsp;
iPhone launched, developers would put together a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.960 --> 00:03:00.000
jailbreak for it. And just a few weeks after this,&nbsp;
the jailbreaking community would come out with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:05.080
world’s first iPhone game called Lights Off.&nbsp;
Lights Off was a very simple straight forward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:10.680
game that never really caught on but it’s what&nbsp;
it represented that really mattered. This game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:15.480
was an assertion from the developer community that&nbsp;
if Apple wasn’t going to directly allow for native&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:21.400
development, they were just gonna do it on their&nbsp;
own, and that’s exactly what happened. In October&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:26.960
of 2007, the community would release an easily&nbsp;
accessible jailbreak called AppSnapp which came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:32.280
with it’s own app store called Installer.app. This&nbsp;
would quickly evolve into Cydia which I’m sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:37.800
you’re all familiar with and it’s what finally got&nbsp;
Steve Jobs off his high horse. The same month that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:42.840
Installer.app was released, Jobs would issue an&nbsp;
open letter to the developer community. He didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:47.240
quite apologize for his previous stance but he&nbsp;
did announce that a native App Store was coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.240 --> 00:03:53.040
very soon. Imagine if Jobs hadn’t relented. Cydia&nbsp;
would’ve became magnitudes larger than it did as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:57.480
a lot more developers would’ve jumped on board.&nbsp;
Apple may have even been forced to support Cydia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.480 --> 00:04:02.200
at one point if it became wide spread enough, but&nbsp;
fortunately for Apple, Jobs made the right choice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:08.840
and launched their own App Store on July 10, 2008&nbsp;
with just over 500 apps. On paper, the launch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.840 --> 00:04:14.120
was a massive success. Within just the first&nbsp;
weekend, the App Store would reach 10 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:19.320
downloads. Just 2 months later, they would reach&nbsp;
100 million downloads, and within just 9 months,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:25.160
they would reach 1 billion downloads. But putting&nbsp;
these insane numbers aside, Apple was actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:36.946
having quite a tough time internally because Steve&nbsp;
had insisted on doing everything the Apple Way.

00:04:36.946 --> 00:04:38.160
THE APPLE WAY:
Despite technically giving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:42.960
developers what they wanted with the app store,&nbsp;
Apple was still receiving a bunch of backlash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:48.080
from developers. Likely the biggest reason for&nbsp;
this was that Apple was forcing developers to use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:53.880
Apple tools to make apps: Xcode and UIKit. Since&nbsp;
then, we have seen alternatives and more neutral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:59.600
languages pop up like Flutter but the bread and&nbsp;
butter of iOS development is still Xcode which a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:05.160
lot of developers are still quite salty about.&nbsp;
In fact, that’s why Xcode has a relatively low&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:11.920
rating of 3.1 stars. It’s not that Xcode is bad&nbsp;
per se but simply that a lot of developers are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:17.240
still pissed off that they’re forced to use it.&nbsp;
But, this is issue with developers was the least&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.240 --> 00:05:22.440
of Apple’s concerns when it came to the App Store.&nbsp;
Likely their biggest issue was actually supporting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:28.280
the insane scale of iOS development. With&nbsp;
something like Windows, this was never a problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:33.400
because Microsoft doesn’t really control what can&nbsp;
be installed on Windows machines. So, all they had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:38.880
to do was create an open platform and support&nbsp;
a broad range of implementations. Everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:45.160
else was up to developers whether it be testing or&nbsp;
distribution or scale. The same however could not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.160 --> 00:05:50.360
be said about the App Store. You see, while Apple&nbsp;
allows developers to make apps for their various&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.360 --> 00:05:57.720
devices, iOS is still not an open platform. It’s&nbsp;
more of a quasi open platform as every single line&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:03.040
of code that makes its way on to the App Store&nbsp;
has to be manually reviewed by Apple. On one side,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.040 --> 00:06:08.840
this largely limits what developers can and cannot&nbsp;
do, and that’s why jailbreaking and Cydia were so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:13.600
big despite the App Store. On the other side,&nbsp;
this ensures that everything on the App Store&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:18.600
is of a certain level of quality and doesn’t&nbsp;
contain malware or viruses or anything harmful.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:24.920
That’s why all iOS apps have certain aura about&nbsp;
them when it comes to UI and completeness. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.920 --> 00:06:31.520
this also means that Apple has to manually review&nbsp;
every single version of every single app. Yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:36.000
I think you can see why that would be rather&nbsp;
hard to manage, and Apple doesn’t take these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:40.440
reviews lightly either. Of course, you’ll hear&nbsp;
about something that slipped through the cracks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:45.520
here and there. For example, they once accidently&nbsp;
approved an app that would straight up let users&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.520 --> 00:06:51.600
jailbreak their iPhone, but for the most part,&nbsp;
Apple’s review system is very robust. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:56.920
40% of App Store submissions are rejected&nbsp;
largely due to nitpicky things. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.920 --> 00:07:02.200
a keyboard might’ve been partially covering up&nbsp;
a textfield which made the UI not as pristine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.200 --> 00:07:06.400
Sometimes Apple will even reject an app on the&nbsp;
basis that they don’t think its valuable to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:11.640
users. But not only does Apple pride themselves&nbsp;
in picking out the smallest of mistakes but they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.640 --> 00:07:18.320
aim to do it within 24-48 hours. Yeah, I don’t&nbsp;
think you’d be surprised to hear that Apple has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:24.600
500 people working full time to review iOS apps.&nbsp;
But not only is the App Store labor intensive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:29.800
it’s also capital intensive. Going back to Windows&nbsp;
for example, if you wanna download Photoshop, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.800 --> 00:07:34.760
would head to Adobe’s website, and it would be up&nbsp;
to Adobe to ensure that their website and servers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.760 --> 00:07:40.720
can scale to the demand. With the App Store,&nbsp;
however, it’s all up to Apple. Aka, they currently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:47.720
have to support 142 billion downloads every single&nbsp;
year. With the average iOS app being 34 MB, we’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.720 --> 00:07:54.400
talking about 4.8 exabytes of data or 4.8 million&nbsp;
terabytes worth of downloads every single year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.400 --> 00:08:00.320
Clearly, following the Apple way with the App&nbsp;
Store has cost Apple quite a bit, but fortunately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:12.346
there was gold at the end of the rainbow which&nbsp;
brings us into the economics of the App Store.

00:08:12.346 --> 00:08:14.800
THE APPLE TAX:
Apple has a few ways of monetizing the App&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:21.360
Store starting with directly charging developers.&nbsp;
Every year, each developer has to pay Apple $100.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:26.760
Technically, there is a free tier that you could&nbsp;
use to mess around with coding iOS apps, but if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.760 --> 00:08:32.360
you actually want your app to end up on the App&nbsp;
Store, you gotta have the paid tier. As of 2022,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.360 --> 00:08:37.240
Apple had a total of 34 million registered&nbsp;
developers. How many of these developers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.240 --> 00:08:41.840
are on the paid tier is unclear, but it’s very&nbsp;
possible that this fee alone generates over a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.840 --> 00:08:47.280
billion dollars in annual revenue for Apple. Given&nbsp;
how much Apple makes off the App Store though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:54.440
this pales in comparison which brings us into&nbsp;
Apple’s main mode of monetization: the Apple tax.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.440 --> 00:09:00.400
For apps that generate less than $1 million per&nbsp;
year, developers have to pay 15% to Apple. And for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:06.560
apps that generate more than $1 million per year,&nbsp;
developers have to pay 30%. Recently, these fees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:11.800
have gotten a lot of criticism as being the 30%&nbsp;
Apple tax that’s applied to all revenue that goes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.800 --> 00:09:19.280
through the App Store, but that’s not quite how it&nbsp;
works. I mean think about it, $1.1 trillion worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.280 --> 00:09:24.920
of revenue goes through the App Store. If Apple&nbsp;
charged 30% on all of that, that itself would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.920 --> 00:09:31.920
$330 billion worth of revenue or a wholenother $3&nbsp;
trillion company. While the App Store is extremely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:38.080
lucrative, it’s no where near that lucrative.&nbsp;
In reality, Apple actually only makes $20 to $25&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.080 --> 00:09:43.920
billion per year from the App Store. So, in terms&nbsp;
of percentages, they’re only earning 2 to 2.5% of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:49.880
the revenue that passes through the App Store. And&nbsp;
this is how Apple largely defends their 30% fee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.880 --> 00:09:54.720
but that’s not to say that the fee is small&nbsp;
like Apple claims either. The reality is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.720 --> 00:10:00.800
Apple’s fee is quite large but not to the people&nbsp;
that you might think. Take Amazon, for example.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:04.880
They don’t release official revenue stats for&nbsp;
their desktop website vs their mobile app, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.880 --> 00:10:10.720
according to sellers, it’s usually about a 60/40&nbsp;
split with the majority coming from mobile. Aka,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.720 --> 00:10:16.280
Amazon pulls in nearly $300 billion every year&nbsp;
thanks to their iOS and Android apps. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.280 --> 00:10:22.480
it’s not like they’re paying $100 billion to Apple&nbsp;
and Google who by the way has nearly an identical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.480 --> 00:10:28.080
pricing model with the Play Store. How do you they&nbsp;
avoid this massive fee you ask? Well, it’s not due&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.080 --> 00:10:33.560
to some sort of secret background deal though&nbsp;
they do have that as well. The real secret is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.560 --> 00:10:39.080
simply that Amazon has the authority to directly&nbsp;
charge customers. Almost everyone has their credit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.080 --> 00:10:44.840
card directly linked with Amazon, so Amazon has&nbsp;
virtually no need Apple’s payment infrastructure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.840 --> 00:10:49.920
which is where the Apple tax actually applies.&nbsp;
It’s basically the same case with every other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:55.080
big company or financial platform. These guys&nbsp;
can charge you directly, so they don’t have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.080 --> 00:11:00.600
to deal with the Apple tax. The only notable&nbsp;
exception to this rule is streaming services.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.600 --> 00:11:06.520
People often subscribe to streaming services out&nbsp;
of impulse, and these streaming giants don’t want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.520 --> 00:11:11.360
to lose out on customers by slowing down the&nbsp;
process and asking for credit card details. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.360 --> 00:11:16.520
they’ll often just use Apple’s double click to pay&nbsp;
stuff. With that being said, you should be careful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.520 --> 00:11:21.520
when subscribing to these services through Apple&nbsp;
because it’s often more expensive. YouTube Premium&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.520 --> 00:11:26.560
for example normally costs $14 per month. But, if&nbsp;
you buy it through the app using Apple’s payment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.560 --> 00:11:32.600
infrastructure, it’s $19 per month to account for&nbsp;
the 30% tax. So, all of the big companies that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.600 --> 00:11:37.240
account for the vast majority of the $1 trillion&nbsp;
going through the App Store don’t even have to pay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.240 --> 00:11:42.800
Apple a dime if they don’t want to. The people who&nbsp;
actually end up paying Apple boat load is smaller&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.800 --> 00:11:47.360
developers who are solely dependent on Apple’s&nbsp;
payment infrastructure which is usually game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.360 --> 00:11:52.680
developers. People like RobTop from Geometry Dash,&nbsp;
Supercell from Clash of Clans and Clash Royale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.680 --> 00:11:58.000
or Nintendo who have been trying to fight&nbsp;
the Apple tax for years. It seems that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:03.280
Microsoft has also joined this fight which is not&nbsp;
surprising given their recent gaming acquisitions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.280 --> 00:12:09.920
but the Apple tax still stands. Even if the Apple&nbsp;
tax eventually comes down though, while it would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.920 --> 00:12:16.000
be a nice break for smaller developers, it doesn’t&nbsp;
really matter for Apple because in the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:21.120
it doesn’t really change how powerful the&nbsp;
Apple App Store really is. With historically&nbsp;&nbsp;

